<article class="env-lemma" id="0HB3">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0HB3">Lemma <span data-tag="0HB3">110.10.1</span>.</a> Let $I \subset R$ be an ideal of a ring. Let $M$ be an $I$-adically complete $R$-module and let $N \subset M$ be a submodule. The following are equivalent </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$N$ is closed in $M$, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$N = \bigcap _{n \geq 1} (N + I^ n M)$, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$M/N$ is $I$-adically complete 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
  <p> If $I$ is finitely generated, these conditions imply that $N$ is $I$-adically complete. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      The equivalence of (1) and (2) follows from Formal Spaces, Lemma <a href="/tag/0AMS" data-tag="0AMS">87.4.2</a>. The equivalence of (2) and (3) is Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/031A" data-tag="031A">10.96.10</a>. Assume $I$ is finitely generated and (1) holds. Let $N^\wedge $ be the $I$-adic completion of $N$. Since $M$ is $I$-adically complete we have a factorization 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  N \to N^\wedge \to M  \]
</div>
    <p>
       of the inclusion map. Since $N^\wedge \to M$ is continuous and $N$ is dense in $N^\wedge $ and $N$ is closed in $M$, we see that $N^\wedge \to M$ factors through $N$. Thus $N^\wedge = N \oplus C$ for some $A$-module $C$. Thus $N$ is a direct summand of the $I$-adically complete module $N^\wedge $, see Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/05GG" data-tag="05GG">10.96.3</a> (this is where we use that $I$ is finitely generated), and hence $N$ is $I$-adically complete. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>